th] 6.42.$ What is the price per pound?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit price, we divide the total cost by the number of pounds:\n\n$$\n6.42  \\div 3 = 2.14\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $\\[math] 2.14$ per pound." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-60ccce15-6e30-58e5-a087-52100ebb460b" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "60ccce15-6e30-58e5-a087-52100ebb460b" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-f60f0711-fce9-525f-a2d1-fdf86ccef7b9" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-05c8cffe-2eeb-5afe-9742-13e5b3b2c630" "question-1f900baf-6fb2-5ced-9586-c12d0492133f" "question-9154bcfc-535d-5b52-8c6a-5ddc0039e572" "question-5fae9bf2-7c1b-5d11-aed5-585d2966343f" "question-b84fb10f-3175-5407-8a39-607c3eb8079e"] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Smaller Units" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f60f0711-fce9-525f-a2d1-fdf86ccef7b9" :question/id #uuid "f60f0711-fce9-525f-a2d1-fdf86ccef7b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4541" :question/problem "A $5$-gallon jug of water costs $\\[math] 7.60.$ What is the price per quart?" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert from gallons to quarts. There are $4$ quarts in each gallon, so we multiply the number of gallons by $4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n5 \\times 4 = 20\n$$\n\nSo $5$ gallons is the same as $20$ quarts. Now, to find the price per quart, we divide the total price by $20\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n7.60 \\div 20 = 0.38\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water costs $\\[math] 0.38$ per quart." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "knowledge-point-247d98e4-8f13-5536-8ad6-00a14e476caf" :knowledge-point/id #uuid "247d98e4-8f13-5536-8ad6-00a14e476caf" :knowledge-point/canonical-example "question-13572493-e244-5164-b0c2-a6c101cfeecc" :knowledge-point/questions ["question-80687806-40a5-5de4-a9f6-85c3a9e00b61" "question-af7b9054-324a-5b9b-bc1b-f5ed085967ba" "question-a7e5dcb2-f757-5065-9783-df93e6eff2aa" "question-b7148b05-c37b-56db-9e21-9c51d5a86ad3" "question-439aee16-5dee-54f4-90f1-94c1e1c52d92"] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Unit Price With Conversion to Larger Units" :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-13572493-e244-5164-b0c2-a6c101cfeecc" :question/id #uuid "13572493-e244-5164-b0c2-a6c101cfeecc" :question/math-academy-id "e-22088" :question/problem "A bottle contains $32$ fluid ounces of juice and costs $\\[math]8.00.$ What's the unit price per cup?\n\nAssume that there are $8$ fluid ounces in one cup." :question/worked-solution "First, we convert from fluid ounces to cups. There are $8$ fluid ounces in each cup, so we divide the number of fluid ounces by $8\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n32 \\div 8 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, $32$ fluid ounces is the same as $4$ cups.  \n\nNow, to find the unit price per cup, we divide the total price by $4\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n8.00 \\div 4 = 2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the juice costs $\\[math]2.00$ per cup." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-577e3bd3-7831-5697-9d2d-c928a9f97262" :question/id #uuid "577e3bd3-7831-5697-9d2d-c928a9f97262" :question/math-academy-id "q-68490" :question/problem "A bag of $50$ medium sized apples costs $$25.$ What's the unit price per apple?" :question/worked-solution "To find the unit price per apple, we divide the total cost by the number of apples:\n\n$$\n25÷50=0.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit price is $$0.50$ per apple." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15b96b95-a002-50f6-8375-1e86f1b96b0c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15b96b95-a002-50f6-8375-1e86f1b96b0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "15b96b95-a002-50f6-8375-1e86f1b96b0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07594779-0625-5b1a-9c55-adbdf283f05c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d275c527-c5de-5384-b5b0-33c29f7b372c" "answer-14fe7288-e5ff-5569-9873-bffc90025dfe" "answer-07594779-0625-5b1a-9c55-adbdf283f05c" "answer-3d6266c0-7544-5287-9fb7-0ee7584e3e17" "an