raduation ceremony, and his total bill comes to $10(5R+4V)$ dollars.\n\nWhat does the factor $10$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "Five roses cost $5R$ dollars and four violets cost $4V$ dollars. Thus, the cost, in dollars, of a bouquet is\n\n$$\n5R+4V.\n$$\n\nJoseph's bill, in dollars, comes to\n\n$$\n10(5R+4V).\n$$\n\nThis is $10$ times the cost of a single bouquet. Thus, the factor $10$ must be the total number of bouquets that Joseph purchased." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64076f1e-6086-54b3-8b25-3b9094d37ea2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64076f1e-6086-54b3-8b25-3b9094d37ea2" :answer-field/id #uuid "64076f1e-6086-54b3-8b25-3b9094d37ea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c50ef978-77e4-51b9-bd70-a113b385c423" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35494911-5d01-5530-9a0f-21709cabf29b" "answer-fbabd838-2512-570b-a671-37bfb2ed2110" "answer-c50ef978-77e4-51b9-bd70-a113b385c423" "answer-5c6f582f-d7ad-528e-85fb-0bed97c8e22e" "answer-09311d83-111f-5567-b483-3659faf7b6b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35494911-5d01-5530-9a0f-21709cabf29b" :answer/id #uuid "35494911-5d01-5530-9a0f-21709cabf29b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each bouquet costs $$10$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbabd838-2512-570b-a671-37bfb2ed2110" :answer/id #uuid "fbabd838-2512-570b-a671-37bfb2ed2110" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each bouquet costs $5R+4V$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c50ef978-77e4-51b9-bd70-a113b385c423" :answer/id #uuid "c50ef978-77e4-51b9-bd70-a113b385c423" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Joseph purchased $10$ bouquets in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c6f582f-d7ad-528e-85fb-0bed97c8e22e" :answer/id #uuid "5c6f582f-d7ad-528e-85fb-0bed97c8e22e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are $10$ roses in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09311d83-111f-5567-b483-3659faf7b6b6" :answer/id #uuid "09311d83-111f-5567-b483-3659faf7b6b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are $10$ violets in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5846d998-51e9-574a-9a51-5526adf86d55" :question/id #uuid "5846d998-51e9-574a-9a51-5526adf86d55" :question/math-academy-id "q-45109" :question/problem "A small pizza costs $S$ dollars and a medium drink costs $M$ dollars. Kate is purchasing some pizzas and drinks for her family and the total bill comes to $6S+3M$ dollars. \n\nWhat does the factor $3$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "A small pizza costs $S$ dollars and a medium drink costs $M$ dollars.\n\nKate's bill, in dollars, comes to\n\n$$\n6S+3M.\n$$\n\nHence, the factor $3$ must be the total number of medium drinks that Kate purchased." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4b8781f3-146c-58fd-b134-089f69cd061b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4b8781f3-146c-58fd-b134-089f69cd061b" :answer-field/id #uuid "4b8781f3-146c-58fd-b134-089f69cd061b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b5da7c72-ce33-58f8-a230-521f31aafe24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8eeae1a-9a77-510d-8565-6cea9fd82a78" "answer-6d5cee32-6e9f-59a4-aabb-e7a461e0f774" "answer-9ac2f850-63dd-5fd8-b6fc-4903951c7324" "answer-cd3e3a82-a892-5c25-9f44-4e2fe9aee571" "answer-b5da7c72-ce33-58f8-a230-521f31aafe24"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8eeae1a-9a77-510d-8565-6cea9fd82a78" :answer/id #uuid "a8eeae1a-9a77-510d-8565-6cea9fd82a78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each medium drink costs $$3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d5cee32-6e9f-59a4-aabb-e7a461e0f774" :answer/id #uuid "6d5cee32-6e9f-59a4-aabb-e7a461e0f774" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each small pizza costs $$3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ac2f850-63dd-5fd8-b6fc-4903951c7324" :answer/id #uuid "9ac2f850-63dd-5fd8-b6fc-4903951c7324" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Kate has purchased $3M$ medium drinks in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3e3a82-a892-5c25-9f44-4e2fe9aee571" :answer/id #uuid "cd3e3a82-a892-5c25-9f44-4e2fe9aee571" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Kate has purchased $M$ medium drinks in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5da7c72-ce33-58f8-a230-521f31aafe24" :answer/id #uuid "b5da7c72-ce33-58f8-a230-521f31aafe24" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The total number of medium drinks that Kate purchased" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-261e2c7f-a2b0-5f74-8412-23a24f65f2b4" :question/id #uuid "261e2c7f-a2b0-5f74-8412-23a24f65f2b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110315" :question/problem "In a cinema, a snack combo consists of four hot dogs, two medium fries, and two medium soft drinks. The cost of one hot dog is $H$ dollars, the cost of one portion of fries is $F$ dollars and the cost of one drink is $D$ dollars. Alan is purchasing some combos for his friends and the total bill comes to $5(4H+2F+2D)$ dollars. \n\nWhat does the factor $5$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "A hot dog costs $H$ dollars, one medium fries costs $F$ dollars, and a medium soft drink costs $D$ dollars. Thus, the cost, in dollars, of a combo is\n\n$$\n4H+2F+2D.\n$$\n\nAlan's bill, in dollars, comes to\n\n$$\n5(4H+2F+2D).\n$$\n\nHence, the factor $5$ must be the total number of combos that he's purchased." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f9a522c8-2da3-5f8b-a8d6-4f543f912c76"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f9a522c8-2da3-5f8b-a8d6-4f543f912c76" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9a522c8-2da3-5f8b-a8d6-4f543f912c76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82bfcd99-5fc7-5938-84a4-efbaa0587f7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7a736020-45d4-5b30-8e67-8fd11f5dd934" "answer-ab423b52-1289-5966-a9dd-1630efd13913" "answer-82bfcd99-5fc7-5938-84a4-efbaa0587f7e" "answer-7d758c08-bea6-544c-a1c3-527e884ef9c5" "answer-35a70b18-5978-500c-a989-bdd2a58ce009"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a736020-45d4-5b30-8e67-8fd11f5dd934" :answer/id #uuid "7a736020-45d4-5b30-8e67-8fd11f5dd934" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Alan has purchased $4H+2F+2D$ hot dogs in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab423b52-1289-5966-a9dd-1630efd13913" :answer/id #uuid "ab423b52-1289-5966-a9dd-1630efd13913" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Alan has purchased $5(4H+2F+2D)$ combos in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82bfcd99-5fc7-5938-84a4-efbaa0587f7e" :answer/id #uuid "82bfcd99-5fc7-5938-84a4-efbaa0587f7e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Alan purchased $5$ combos in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d758c08-bea6-544c-a1c3-527e884ef9c5" :answer/id #uuid "7d758c08-bea6-544c-a1c3-527e884ef9c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each combo costs $5(4H+2F+2D)$ dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35a70b18-5978-500c-a989-bdd2a58ce009" :answer/id #uuid "35a70b18-5978-500c-a989-bdd2a58ce009" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each hot dog costs $$5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7d200e6-96cf-5acb-92f7-781b4bd31a5f" :question/id #uuid "f7d200e6-96cf-5acb-92f7-781b4bd31a5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110316" :question/problem "A bouquet of flowers consists of $8$ tulips and $4$ violets. The cost of one tulip is $T$ dollars and  the cost of one violet is $V$ dollars. Thomas is purchasing some bouquets for a wedding, and his total bill comes to $12(8T+4V)$ dollars.\n\nWhat does the factor $12$ mean in this context?" :question/worked-solution "Eight tulips cost $8T$ dollars, and four violets cost $4V$ dollars. Thus, the cost,  in dollars, of a bouquet is\n\n$$\n8T+4V.\n$$\n\nThomas' bill,  in dollars, comes to\n\n$$\n12(8T+4V).\n$$\n\nThis is $12$ times the cost of a single bouquet. Thus, the factor $12$ must be the total number of bouquets that Thomas purchased." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-37240545-1fe3-5f0d-b1eb-5faf520bf81f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-37240545-1fe3-5f0d-b1eb-5faf520bf81f" :answer-field/id #uuid "37240545-1fe3-5f0d-b1eb-5faf520bf81f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-644db01d-40c6-53ba-8fcc-1f1f62dc79c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-01bd6103-2e3a-5532-8ccc-de057f9bbd7f" "answer-9a5d3a56-e003-54c8-9d7d-248fb5af6f56" "answer-8fec6a6e-5778-585f-836e-300c1705088b" "answer-a04cf854-380f-54c5-bd5d-e1774bc4156b" "answer-644db01d-40c6-53ba-8fcc-1f1f62dc79c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-01bd6103-2e3a-5532-8ccc-de057f9bbd7f" :answer/id #uuid "01bd6103-2e3a-5532-8ccc-de057f9bbd7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each bouquet costs $$12$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a5d3a56-e003-54c8-9d7d-248fb5af6f56" :answer/id #uuid "9a5d3a56-e003-54c8-9d7d-248fb5af6f56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Each bouquet costs $12(8T+4V)$  dollars" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fec6a6e-5778-585f-836e-300c1705088b" :answer/id #uuid "8fec6a6e-5778-585f-836e-300c1705088b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are $12$ tulips in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a04cf854-380f-54c5-bd5d-e1774bc4156b" :answer/id #uuid "a04cf854-380f-54c5-bd5d-e1774bc4156b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There are $12$ violets in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-644db01d-40c6-53ba-8fcc-1f1f62dc79c5" :answer/id #uuid "644db01d-40c6-53ba-8fcc-1f1f62dc79c5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Thomas purchased $12$ bouquets in total" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135012 :tutorial/content "The expression below gives the cost, in dollars, of sending a letter to Spain: \n\n$$\n2w + 1\n$$\n\nHere, $w$ is the letter's weight in ounces.\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\:{\\color{blue}2}\\!\\!\\!{\\underset{\\underset{\\Large\\textrm{weight}}{\\large\\uparrow}}{w}}\\!\\!\\!+{\\color{red}1}\\:}^{\\large\\textrm{total cost}}\n$$\n\nThe coefficient of $w$ is the cost for each additional ounce of weight.\n\nTo illustrate:\n\n- If $w=1$ (we have a $1$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 1 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]3.$\n\n- If $w=2$ (we have a $2$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 2 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[/math]5.$\n\n- If $w=3$ (we have a $3$-ounce letter), the cost is ${\\color{blue}2} \\cdot 3 + {\\color{red}1} = \\[math]7.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The number $\\color{blue}2$ in this expression represents a *unit rate.* Each time we increase the letter's weight by $1$ ounce, the cost increases by $\\color{blue}\\[/math]2$.\n\n- There is also a **base fee** of $\\color{red}\\[math]1$, which does not depend on the weight of the letter. We can think of this as the cost of sending a zero-ounce letter." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186135149 :tutorial/content "In many countries, a **sales tax** is charged whenever an individual or company makes a purchase. The tax is usually based on a certain percentage of the sale price.\n\nFor example, suppose a person purchases a bicycle for $\\[math] 200,$ and there is a sales tax of $5\\%.$ How much is this tax, and what is the total cost of the bicycle?\n\nTo determine the amount of the tax the person must pay, we multiply $200 \\times 5\\%$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tax & =\\text{\\[}/math]200\\times 5% \\\\  & =\\text{\\[}math]200\\times 0.05 \\\\  & =\\text{\\[}/math]10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the purchaser must pay a $\\[math]10$ sales tax. This means that the total amount the person needs to pay for the bicycle is\n\n$$\n\\[/math]200 + \\[math]10 = \\[/math]210.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186134048 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186198373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" "question-11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375103 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+y\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+y$ and $v=y,$ the integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{(\\frac{u-v}{2})}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186375509 :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" :question/id #uuid "75903bf3-d1b7-5813-a3cf-49663583fcbd" :question/math-academy-id "q-160915" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le xy\\le 4,\\,1\\le \\frac{y}{x}\\le 5,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=xy\\,\\text{and}\\,v=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }y & x \\\\ -\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{2y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2y}{x})}=\\frac{1}{2v}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{D}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)|\\frac{1}{2v}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)[{\\int }_{1}^{5}v\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u){[\\frac{1}{2}\\,{v}^{2}]}_{1}^{5}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\cdot 12\\,du \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi u)\\,du \\\\  & =6{[-\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi u)]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{\\pi }(1-(-1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "caaee621-928c-5865-8689-d0a675bcc7ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" "answer-377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" "answer-389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" "answer-36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" "answer-adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/id #uuid "76f8296f-42f1-5d17-a5c6-7274b014898c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" :answer/id #uuid "377131c4-9276-5492-90f4-5fe9f9ea094b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" :answer/id #uuid "389c158d-eca4-5138-ade2-702a2174ac43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3(2\\pi -1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" :answer/id #uuid "36ad6cac-d25a-50df-a71c-ff5530463782" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3-4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a" :answer/id #uuid "adfbcfe0-1ed2-552d-9eab-c92cbe93246a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6" :question/id #uuid "11be1c1c-7eab-52eb-aab8-c16c55806ef6" :question/math-academy-id "q-160917" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n0\\le y-2x\\le 2,\\,2\\le y+2x\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-2x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=y+2x.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,2\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }\\text{ }\\text{ }2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{y-2x}{y+2x}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{u}{v}|-\\frac{1}{4}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[{\\int }_{2}^{6}\\frac{1}{v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|v|]_{2}^{6}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u(\\operatorname{ln}⁡6-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{4}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311" :answer-field/id #uuid "91b5b4ea-2fbf-58c6-b385-fcfece3b9311" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" "answer-ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" "answer-b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" "answer-83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" "answer-807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" :answer/id #uuid "76a2fca9-a004-56dc-bcb9-7a9d56bf96b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" :answer/id #uuid "ebf26341-3745-5f43-a14c-cda67d69e390" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" :answer/id #uuid "b4dca2c1-f3a5-573b-82d7-9841c885f465" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/id #uuid "83e55904-4dad-5f50-9ed8-a699c5c350a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c" :answer/id #uuid "807c89b0-1baf-5d8a-afd4-cecf678b8f6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186373930 :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(x-1){e}^{2x+2y}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(u-1){e}^{2v}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)[{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{e}^{2v}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1){[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2v}]}_{-1}^{1}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(u-1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{2}-u]}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133963 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cd9fe40e-bc91-5a44-83a1-8324033e7c36" "question-ea15f44d-2e1d-56cb-9675-b5eb8b0bd610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd9fe40e-bc91-5a44-83a1-8324033e7c36" :question/id #uuid "cd9fe40e-bc91-5a44-83a1-8324033e7c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-41396" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{4p-1}{2(2p+2)}$ for $p=4.$" :question/worked-solution "We replace $p$ with $4$ in the above expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4p-1}{2(2p+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(4)-1}{2(2(4)+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{2(8+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{15}{20} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ae640502-6ec1-5965-8f84-422b9cf2d36f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ae640502-6ec1-5965-8f84-422b9cf2d36f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ae640502-6ec1-5965-8f84-422b9cf2d36f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c99f603d-0d8c-5cad-a745-389ae3790563" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f95e908-cc3c-52a0-b192-0d0710737e7b" "answer-1e3dbef9-dc90-5521-aad3-0e986ff0a9c9" "answer-c99f603d-0d8c-5cad-a745-389ae3790563" "answer-fc53b599-5c2a-51eb-849a-b5560be90857" "answer-8afd3cea-dcab-580e-a906-ca6e1b3ef1ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f95e908-cc3c-52a0-b192-0d0710737e7b" :answer/id #uuid "2f95e908-cc3c-52a0-b192-0d0710737e7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e3dbef9-dc90-5521-aad3-0e986ff0a9c9" :answer/id #uuid "1e3dbef9-dc90-5521-aad3-0e986ff0a9c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c99f603d-0d8c-5cad-a745-389ae3790563" :answer/id #uuid "c99f603d-0d8c-5cad-a745-389ae3790563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc53b599-5c2a-51eb-849a-b5560be90857" :answer/id #uuid "fc53b599-5c2a-51eb-849a-b5560be90857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8afd3cea-dcab-580e-a906-ca6e1b3ef1ef" :answer/id #uuid "8afd3cea-dcab-580e-a906-ca6e1b3ef1ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ea15f44d-2e1d-56cb-9675-b5eb8b0bd610" :question/id #uuid "ea15f44d-2e1d-56cb-9675-b5eb8b0bd610" :question/math-academy-id "q-51597" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{p+9}{2(3-p)}$ for $p=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "We replace $p$ with $-5$ in the above expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{p+9}{2(3-p)} & = \\\\ \\frac{(-5)+9}{2(3-(-5))} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{2(8)} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{16} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ecea4696-381b-5caf-8ad2-71fa9ec32665"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ecea4696-381b-5caf-8ad2-71fa9ec32665" :answer-field/id #uuid "ecea4696-381b-5caf-8ad2-71fa9ec32665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-506d0e3d-76e7-56a6-8950-11ce3311fb87" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3624078e-face-5369-96cf-5bd66283c3d5" "answer-16d21786-f17d-5f51-b039-3a8847d6393b" "answer-506d0e3d-76e7-56a6-8950-11ce3311fb87" "answer-899713f6-4503-506c-8e51-5d19404c5088" "answer-80029657-b251-5c82-b8aa-9552f47c932e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3624078e-face-5369-96cf-5bd66283c3d5" :answer/id #uuid "3624078e-face-5369-96cf-5bd66283c3d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16d21786-f17d-5f51-b039-3a8847d6393b" :answer/id #uuid "16d21786-f17d-5f51-b039-3a8847d6393b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-506d0e3d-76e7-56a6-8950-11ce3311fb87" :answer/id #uuid "506d0e3d-76e7-56a6-8950-11ce3311fb87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-899713f6-4503-506c-8e51-5d19404c5088" :answer/id #uuid "899713f6-4503-506c-8e51-5d19404c5088" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80029657-b251-5c82-b8aa-9552f47c932e" :answer/id #uuid "80029657-b251-5c82-b8aa-9552f47c932e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133965 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ae15419-ea90-5da7-9981-972449aabeb9" "question-8ab342b9-3bac-54fe-9ede-058c11e77bac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ae15419-ea90-5da7-9981-972449aabeb9" :question/id #uuid "1ae15419-ea90-5da7-9981-972449aabeb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-70122" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{2m}{n+4}-\\frac{m-2}{2n}$ if $m=4$ and $n=1$ ." :question/worked-solution "We replace $m$ with $4$ and $n$ with $1$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2m}{n+4}-\\frac{m-2}{2n} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(4)}{1+4}-\\frac{4-2}{2(1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{2}{2} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}-1 & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{5}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{8-5}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eab9a6ff-5e82-586a-84ee-0529550dee14"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eab9a6ff-5e82-586a-84ee-0529550dee14" :answer-field/id #uuid "eab9a6ff-5e82-586a-84ee-0529550dee14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ada5c571-4b79-5699-bf57-b91b3d06e08b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5a193d30-2678-5290-8227-48f94178684b" "answer-71b0bb4d-c696-553e-85b2-c82a397e838c" "answer-e10238d5-b4fc-50dd-852e-f9c92340056d" "answer-d7b1df03-83f7-505b-93fd-e466c959a197" "answer-ada5c571-4b79-5699-bf57-b91b3d06e08b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a193d30-2678-5290-8227-48f94178684b" :answer/id #uuid "5a193d30-2678-5290-8227-48f94178684b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71b0bb4d-c696-553e-85b2-c82a397e838c" :answer/id #uuid "71b0bb4d-c696-553e-85b2-c82a397e838c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e10238d5-b4fc-50dd-852e-f9c92340056d" :answer/id #uuid "e10238d5-b4fc-50dd-852e-f9c92340056d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7b1df03-83f7-505b-93fd-e466c959a197" :answer/id #uuid "d7b1df03-83f7-505b-93fd-e466c959a197" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ada5c571-4b79-5699-bf57-b91b3d06e08b" :answer/id #uuid "ada5c571-4b79-5699-bf57-b91b3d06e08b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133964 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0f15a926-c445-59e0-9b0e-24fb3d26db90"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0f15a926-c445-59e0-9b0e-24fb3d26db90" :question/id #uuid "0f15a926-c445-59e0-9b0e-24fb3d26db90" :question/math-academy-id "q-99830" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{8}{x}+\\frac{2}{4x}$ for $x=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "We replace $x$ with $-5$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{x}+\\frac{2}{4x} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{(-5)}+\\frac{2}{4\\cdot (-5)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{8}{5}+(-\\frac{2}{20}) & = \\\\ -\\frac{8}{5}-\\frac{1}{10} & = \\\\ -\\frac{16}{10}-\\frac{1}{10} & = \\\\ \\frac{-16-1}{10} & = \\\\ -\\frac{17}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-df747204-bd95-5057-9f83-9ee058a01322"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-df747204-bd95-5057-9f83-9ee058a01322" :answer-field/id #uuid "df747204-bd95-5057-9f83-9ee058a01322" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93ee67a5-c7ed-59bc-a55b-a2b9816ca144" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a652b819-cc60-593b-be0f-164896bc2a59" "answer-93ee67a5-c7ed-59bc-a55b-a2b9816ca144" "answer-28e4f656-f65d-5411-8d23-3582c6860ee1" "answer-7e76872b-704c-5f0c-a9ff-08c0e8fdc198" "answer-03fe396d-fe54-5331-9b0d-89cca76e8a83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a652b819-cc60-593b-be0f-164896bc2a59" :answer/id #uuid "a652b819-cc60-593b-be0f-164896bc2a59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{10}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93ee67a5-c7ed-59bc-a55b-a2b9816ca144" :answer/id #uuid "93ee67a5-c7ed-59bc-a55b-a2b9816ca144" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{17}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28e4f656-f65d-5411-8d23-3582c6860ee1" :answer/id #uuid "28e4f656-f65d-5411-8d23-3582c6860ee1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e76872b-704c-5f0c-a9ff-08c0e8fdc198" :answer/id #uuid "7e76872b-704c-5f0c-a9ff-08c0e8fdc198" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03fe396d-fe54-5331-9b0d-89cca76e8a83" :answer/id #uuid "03fe396d-fe54-5331-9b0d-89cca76e8a83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ab342b9-3bac-54fe-9ede-058c11e77bac" :question/id #uuid "8ab342b9-3bac-54fe-9ede-058c11e77bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-175902" :question/problem "If $p=-2$ and $q=6,$ then $\\frac{2q-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2p}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Replacing $p$ with $-2$ and $q$ with $6$ in the expression, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2p} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(6)-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4-2(-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{12-3}{4}+\\frac{4}{4+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{18}{8}+\\frac{4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{18+4}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{22}{8} & = \\\\ \\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-390cda6c-7afa-531f-b8d2-8d4ab6b358f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-390cda6c-7afa-531f-b8d2-8d4ab6b358f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "390cda6c-7afa-531f-b8d2-8d4ab6b358f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-90b1e99c-00a5-5d5c-9c07-a9ac4425d78b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-90b1e99c-00a5-5d5c-9c07-a9ac4425d78b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90b1e99c-00a5-5d5c-9c07-a9ac4425d78b" :answer/id #uuid "90b1e99c-00a5-5d5c-9c07-a9ac4425d78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-6aa5b166-dd8e-5ca2-8b7d-1419def83709" :activity/id #uuid "6aa5b166-dd8e-5ca2-8b7d-1419def83709" :activity/title "Evaluating Rational Expressions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048938 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-7b236cf6-b776-533a-a5d8-6899df32a482" "step-b33d30b4-f26c-5283-a113-5d791c204f7d" "step-21d43eb5-2713-5f23-bd3a-faf668d76610" "step-280ac367-40c7-558a-b5e2-c35117deba67" "step-e3763ce7-1ebb-58a6-9246-c04907a1a3c3"] :activity/first-step "step-7b236cf6-b776-533a-a5d8-6899df32a482" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7b236cf6-b776-533a-a5d8-6899df32a482" :step/id #uuid "7b236cf6-b776-533a-a5d8-6899df32a482" :step/content "question-cd9fe40e-bc91-5a44-83a1-8324033e7c36" :step/next "step-b33d30b4-f26c-5283-a113-5d791c204f7d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b33d30b4-f26c-5283-a113-5d791c204f7d" :step/id #uuid "b33d30b4-f26c-5283-a113-5d791c204f7d" :step/content "question-ea15f44d-2e1d-56cb-9675-b5eb8b0bd610" :step/next "step-21d43eb5-2713-5f23-bd3a-faf668d76610" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-21d43eb5-2713-5f23-bd3a-faf668d76610" :step/id #uuid "21d43eb5-2713-5f23-bd3a-faf668d76610" :step/content "question-1ae15419-ea90-5da7-9981-972449aabeb9" :step/next "step-280ac367-40c7-558a-b5e2-c35117deba67" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-280ac367-40c7-558a-b5e2-c35117deba67" :step/id #uuid "280ac367-40c7-558a-b5e2-c35117deba67" :step/content "question-0f15a926-c445-59e0-9b0e-24fb3d26db90" :step/next "step-e3763ce7-1ebb-58a6-9246-c04907a1a3c3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e3763ce7-1ebb-58a6-9246-c04907a1a3c3" :step/id #uuid "e3763ce7-1ebb-58a6-9246-c04907a1a3c3" :step/content "question-8ab342b9-3bac-54fe-9ede-058c11e77bac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-149f319f-bbde-53de-865d-1514ecb7ea83" "question-836c76f0-231f-583b-b08e-df25ef466c6b" "question-25eac356-264f-523c-b19b-74743af10bc1" "question-5bda691e-a546-5a71-b17f-20d3338df2d9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115144 :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a basketball player scores at least $2$ baskets in a series of $3$ free throws if the probability of scoring a basket on each free throw is $0.55.$\n\nII. The probability of getting $35$ even numbers on $60$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability that a fair spinner with three regions, red, green, and blue, lands on green in $5$ out of $12$ spins." :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=3$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =3\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =1.65 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\dfrac12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=12$ and $p=\\dfrac13.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115316 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b726722f-b10f-58df-8eed-f61e3c1a5880" "question-6b518eb4-0e6a-5c41-b563-8246f9c1143e" "question-bfd541af-299c-58d0-8805-828b25adeb36" "question-ed942805-cf1d-5ddc-afc6-5eb5833ba5cd" "question-d886c0f0-1fbe-59f2-a61e-06cc1aef64d9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115145 :question/problem "In a city, $44\\%$ of the people have blood type A. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $150$ people are picked at random, then $70$ of them will have blood type A. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p = 0.44.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.44 \\\\  & =66 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.44) \\\\  & =84 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (150)(0.44) = 66\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (150)(0.44)(1-0.44) = 36.96.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(66, 36.96).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X = 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X =70) \\approx P(69.5 \\leq Y \\leq 70.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(69.5\\le Y\\le 70.5) & =P(\\frac{69.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{70.5-66}{\\sqrt{36.96}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.58\\le Z\\le 0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.74)-P(Z\\le 0.58) \\\\  & =Φ(0.74)-Φ(0.58) \\\\  & =0.7704-0.7190 \\\\  & =0.0514\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 70)\\approx 0.051.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0528.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115317 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-865aae44-8837-5a5c-9a79-489c7c05763a" "question-036e3017-f74f-5cab-a417-486991609067" "question-fff943eb-16aa-5bd0-bfca-c95efa66ad92" "question-01c1e9f3-92c7-5436-aa3f-7f0248f68a95" "question-212cd403-0726-5b1b-ae69-c8952ab761bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115146 :question/problem "An archer practices firing an arrow at a target $50$ times. The archer has a $47\\%$ chance of hitting the bull's eye on each shot. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the archer hits the bull's eye more than $29$ times but less than $35$ times. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.6 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9452 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9463 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9474 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.7 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9554 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9564 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9573 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 1.8 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9641 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9649 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9656 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.00 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.01 \\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 0.02 \\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.0 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9987 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.1 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9990 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9991 \\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\, 3.2 \\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9993 \\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\, 0.9994 \\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.47.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.47 \\\\  & =23.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.47) \\\\  & =26.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = np = (50)(0.47) = 23.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = np(1-p) = (50)(0.47)(1-0.47) = 12.455.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y \\sim N(23.5, 12.455).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(29 <   X <    35).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <    35) = P(30 \\leq X \\leq 34) \\approx P(29.5 \\leq Y \\leq    34.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(29.5\\le Y<34.5) & =P(\\frac{29.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{34.5-23.5}{\\sqrt{12.455}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.70\\le Z\\le 3.12) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z<1.70) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 3.12)-P(Z\\le 1.70) \\\\  & =Φ(3.12)-Φ(1.70) \\\\  & =0.9991-0.9554 \\\\  & =0.0437\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(29 <   X <  35)  \\approx 0.044.\n$$\n\n**Note:** Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0436.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115991 :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $12$ defective bolts in a box containing $20$ bolts if the probability that an individual bolt is defective is $0.2.$\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player scores $20$ of $30$ penalties if the probability of scoring each penalty is $0.55.$\n\nIII. The probability of getting $9$ fives on $28$ rolls of a $6$-sided die." :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =4 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=30$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =30\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =16.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =30\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =13.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=28$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =28\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186114883 :answer-field/correct 17592186114887 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-149f319f-bbde-53de-865d-1514ecb7ea83" :question/id #uuid "149f319f-bbde-53de-865d-1514ecb7ea83" :question/math-academy-id "q-95715" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of finding more than $120$ people with blood type O+ in a randomly selected sample of $200$ if $53%$ of the population has blood type O+.\n\nII. The probability of finding less than $4$ defective items in a random sample of $100$ items produced by a machine if $2%$ of the items made are defective.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on heads $9$ times out of $15$ coin flips if the probability of a single flip landing on heads is $0.25.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=200$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =200\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =106 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =200\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =94 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=100$ and $p=0.02.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =100\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=15$ and $p=0.25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =3.75 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-40071c65-69af-5622-b441-b1075a6ee30d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-40071c65-69af-5622-b441-b1075a6ee30d" :answer-field/id #uuid "40071c65-69af-5622-b441-b1075a6ee30d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-37c9a7ad-3564-5e24-a66a-604cb6cb332b" "answer-48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" "answer-ba3131fb-d59c-57b2-930f-2bcd721dbc80" "answer-bba92cf1-0f0e-567f-9bd8-3938ce1c0d9b" "answer-d502be3d-c7bc-5b6f-a8e9-be63c39083a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37c9a7ad-3564-5e24-a66a-604cb6cb332b" :answer/id #uuid "37c9a7ad-3564-5e24-a66a-604cb6cb332b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" :answer/id #uuid "48772f91-3e50-57b1-b7f1-d6c0568250c8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba3131fb-d59c-57b2-930f-2bcd721dbc80" :answer/id #uuid "ba3131fb-d59c-57b2-930f-2bcd721dbc80" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bba92cf1-0f0e-567f-9bd8-3938ce1c0d9b" :answer/id #uuid "bba92cf1-0f0e-567f-9bd8-3938ce1c0d9b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d502be3d-c7bc-5b6f-a8e9-be63c39083a0" :answer/id #uuid "d502be3d-c7bc-5b6f-a8e9-be63c39083a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-836c76f0-231f-583b-b08e-df25ef466c6b" :question/id #uuid "836c76f0-231f-583b-b08e-df25ef466c6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95699" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability that a fair spinner with five regions, red, green, yellow, orange, and blue, lands on yellow in $8$ out of $15$ spins.\n\nII. The probability that a soccer player misses a penalty if the probability of scoring the penalty is $0.48.$\n\nIII. The probability that an archer hits the bull's eye more than $10$ times out of $25$ attempts if the probability of hitting the bull's eye on a single shot is $0.56.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =3 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=1$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =1\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.48 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =14 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b3237761-34f1-5ac9-9936-425deb983312"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b3237761-34f1-5ac9-9936-425deb983312" :answer-field/id #uuid "b3237761-34f1-5ac9-9936-425deb983312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea" :answer-field/choices ["answer-adaf26d5-45a4-5ebe-9d67-93154896144e" "answer-7bfbde10-be8f-5681-9a28-d3fccf5c0410" "answer-5b94ddb7-793b-5a9b-9679-c0ce9e61e2a8" "answer-5f137ed0-57e9-5fca-a4b2-ff2aa08efa47" "answer-3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adaf26d5-45a4-5ebe-9d67-93154896144e" :answer/id #uuid "adaf26d5-45a4-5ebe-9d67-93154896144e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bfbde10-be8f-5681-9a28-d3fccf5c0410" :answer/id #uuid "7bfbde10-be8f-5681-9a28-d3fccf5c0410" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b94ddb7-793b-5a9b-9679-c0ce9e61e2a8" :answer/id #uuid "5b94ddb7-793b-5a9b-9679-c0ce9e61e2a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f137ed0-57e9-5fca-a4b2-ff2aa08efa47" :answer/id #uuid "5f137ed0-57e9-5fca-a4b2-ff2aa08efa47" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea" :answer/id #uuid "3bc12929-212a-53ad-b5cf-1776af111aea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25eac356-264f-523c-b19b-74743af10bc1" :question/id #uuid "25eac356-264f-523c-b19b-74743af10bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-132260" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of getting $5$ twos on $15$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nII. The probability of finding more than $5$ defective pairs of shoes in a random sample of $50$ if $3%$ of the pairs are defective.\n\nIII. The probability of getting more than $40$ \"yes\" answers on a yes/no survey in a randomly selected sample of $150$ people if the probability that a person answers \"yes\" is $0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=15$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.03.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.03 \\\\  & =1.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=150$ and $p=0.5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.5) \\\\  & =75 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6a0fb1b1-4050-5c41-8c18-a8d2d95f7a21"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6a0fb1b1-4050-5c41-8c18-a8d2d95f7a21" :answer-field/id #uuid "6a0fb1b1-4050-5c41-8c18-a8d2d95f7a21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bfaa7fcf-5764-5700-a4a7-b0c0dec4d62f" "answer-f1713091-6aaf-5c7b-b2ae-d9adc05629c3" "answer-b4acbfdf-053d-59b8-ac59-8ef6148fe151" "answer-af92cf95-3e09-52d6-b3b3-48d72e62b0d3" "answer-998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfaa7fcf-5764-5700-a4a7-b0c0dec4d62f" :answer/id #uuid "bfaa7fcf-5764-5700-a4a7-b0c0dec4d62f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1713091-6aaf-5c7b-b2ae-d9adc05629c3" :answer/id #uuid "f1713091-6aaf-5c7b-b2ae-d9adc05629c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4acbfdf-053d-59b8-ac59-8ef6148fe151" :answer/id #uuid "b4acbfdf-053d-59b8-ac59-8ef6148fe151" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af92cf95-3e09-52d6-b3b3-48d72e62b0d3" :answer/id #uuid "af92cf95-3e09-52d6-b3b3-48d72e62b0d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7" :answer/id #uuid "998bd535-075d-5cdc-8946-dcd2da85f0b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5bda691e-a546-5a71-b17f-20d3338df2d9" :question/id #uuid "5bda691e-a546-5a71-b17f-20d3338df2d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95695" :question/problem "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of a student getting $7$ correct answers on a quiz with $10$ questions if each question has $4$ options and the student answers all questions at random.\n\nII. The probability of getting $35$ ones on $60$ rolls of a $6$-sided die.\n\nIII. The probability of having $3$ girls in a family of $7$ children if the probability that a randomly selected child is a girl is $0.48.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nLet's now examine each situation.\n\n- In situation I, we have $n=10$ and $p=0.25.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation II, we have $n=60$ and $p=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =10 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =50 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\n- In situation III, we have $n=7$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =7\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =3.36 \\\\  & ≯5.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we *cannot* use the normal approximation of the binomial distribution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-542b7abb-095b-5961-a340-01b18bc5861a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-542b7abb-095b-5961-a340-01b18bc5861a" :answer-field/id #uuid "542b7abb-095b-5961-a340-01b18bc5861a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6eb8422-bc12-5384-8435-5c69b3ff70cc" "answer-06c65a9f-2e2e-52e5-b194-f2ed8af16558" "answer-5af482ec-476b-5c66-8fed-f5647158a2d8" "answer-b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" "answer-3d7a3324-d339-595f-b7ea-55311864bfea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6eb8422-bc12-5384-8435-5c69b3ff70cc" :answer/id #uuid "a6eb8422-bc12-5384-8435-5c69b3ff70cc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06c65a9f-2e2e-52e5-b194-f2ed8af16558" :answer/id #uuid "06c65a9f-2e2e-52e5-b194-f2ed8af16558" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5af482ec-476b-5c66-8fed-f5647158a2d8" :answer/id #uuid "5af482ec-476b-5c66-8fed-f5647158a2d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" :answer/id #uuid "b72955e4-be8c-54ec-be7d-7e25e01e936f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d7a3324-d339-595f-b7ea-55311864bfea" :answer/id #uuid "3d7a3324-d339-595f-b7ea-55311864bfea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b726722f-b10f-58df-8eed-f61e3c1a5880" :question/id #uuid "b726722f-b10f-58df-8eed-f61e3c1a5880" :question/math-academy-id "q-132275" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(85,0.56),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=50).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=85$ and $p=0.56.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =85\\cdot 0.56 \\\\  & =47.6 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =85\\cdot (1-0.56) \\\\  & =37.4 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(85)(0.56)=47.6\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(85)(0.56)(1-0.56)=20.94.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(47.6,20.94).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=50).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(49.5\\le Y\\le 50.5) & =P(\\frac{49.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{50.5-47.6}{\\sqrt{20.94}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.42\\le Z\\le 0.63) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z<0.42) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.63)-P(Z\\le 0.42) \\\\  & =Φ(0.63)-Φ(0.42) \\\\  & =0.7357-0.6628 \\\\  & =0.073\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=50)≈0.073.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0758.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f507c9e1-30ff-5ca4-a4e4-d03f72191b3d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f507c9e1-30ff-5ca4-a4e4-d03f72191b3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "f507c9e1-30ff-5ca4-a4e4-d03f72191b3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87c0932e-63f4-5bb4-a48c-af20cb5ab96a" "answer-52c6a4e9-74ca-5582-b0af-97d3ec19601a" "answer-821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" "answer-f90fc399-9a07-50e3-a059-9866e338d3fe" "answer-eaf79bda-c742-597d-9104-da468519214c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87c0932e-63f4-5bb4-a48c-af20cb5ab96a" :answer/id #uuid "87c0932e-63f4-5bb4-a48c-af20cb5ab96a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.066" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c6a4e9-74ca-5582-b0af-97d3ec19601a" :answer/id #uuid "52c6a4e9-74ca-5582-b0af-97d3ec19601a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.070" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" :answer/id #uuid "821cd6eb-46cd-5eb8-b1a0-1a9aa204f81f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.073" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f90fc399-9a07-50e3-a059-9866e338d3fe" :answer/id #uuid "f90fc399-9a07-50e3-a059-9866e338d3fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.079" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eaf79bda-c742-597d-9104-da468519214c" :answer/id #uuid "eaf79bda-c742-597d-9104-da468519214c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.082" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b518eb4-0e6a-5c41-b563-8246f9c1143e" :question/id #uuid "6b518eb4-0e6a-5c41-b563-8246f9c1143e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132269" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(150,0.48),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=67).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ | $\\,0.1762\\,$ | $\\,0.1736\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | $\\,0.2033\\,$ | $\\,0.2005\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2297\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=150$ and $p=0.48.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =150\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =150\\cdot (1-0.48) \\\\  & =78 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(150)(0.48)=72\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(150)(0.48)(1-0.48)=37.44.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(72,37.44).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=67).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(66.5\\le Y\\le 67.5) & =P(\\frac{66.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{67.5-72}{\\sqrt{37.44}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.90\\le Z\\le -0.74) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z<-0.90) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -0.74)-P(Z\\le -0.90) \\\\  & =Φ(-0.74)-Φ(-0.90) \\\\  & =0.2297-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.046\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=67)≈0.046.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0468.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee77bc02-a4f1-5565-b42d-e9a68c8e93d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee77bc02-a4f1-5565-b42d-e9a68c8e93d4" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee77bc02-a4f1-5565-b42d-e9a68c8e93d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b203c66c-4318-5382-af46-3a61a328e257" "answer-1b511858-4818-5d57-8bd8-1fe8930c3764" "answer-715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" "answer-8dcc2491-00c7-5189-9306-0c2b27964cda" "answer-5e2bb00f-cdb9-563a-8c96-93286f07eecc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b203c66c-4318-5382-af46-3a61a328e257" :answer/id #uuid "b203c66c-4318-5382-af46-3a61a328e257" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.035" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b511858-4818-5d57-8bd8-1fe8930c3764" :answer/id #uuid "1b511858-4818-5d57-8bd8-1fe8930c3764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.037" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" :answer/id #uuid "715cf66d-11dd-57af-bac0-c97174f16e01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.046" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8dcc2491-00c7-5189-9306-0c2b27964cda" :answer/id #uuid "8dcc2491-00c7-5189-9306-0c2b27964cda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.051" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e2bb00f-cdb9-563a-8c96-93286f07eecc" :answer/id #uuid "5e2bb00f-cdb9-563a-8c96-93286f07eecc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.055" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bfd541af-299c-58d0-8805-828b25adeb36" :question/id #uuid "bfd541af-299c-58d0-8805-828b25adeb36" :question/math-academy-id "q-132294" :question/problem "Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that a soccer player scores $16$ out of $25$ penalties if the player has a $58%$ probability of scoring each penalty. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=25$ and $p=0.58.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =25\\cdot 0.58 \\\\  & =14.5 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =25\\cdot (1-0.58) \\\\  & =10.5 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(25)(0.58)=14.5\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(25)(0.58)(1-0.58)=6.09.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(14.5,6.09).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=16).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=16)≈P(15.5\\le Y\\le 16.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(15.5\\le Y\\le 16.5) & =P(\\frac{15.5-14.5}{\\sqrt{6.09}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{16.5-14.5}{\\sqrt{6.09}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.41\\le Z\\le 0.81) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.81)-P(Z<0.41) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.81)-P(Z\\le 0.41) \\\\  & =Φ(0.81)-Φ(0.41) \\\\  & =0.7910-0.6591 \\\\  & =0.132\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=16)≈0.132.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.1363.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea74ac30-fe03-5372-8e51-7d585346e95d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea74ac30-fe03-5372-8e51-7d585346e95d" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea74ac30-fe03-5372-8e51-7d585346e95d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fa2daa2-a228-5079-83e2-440c95a5759c" "answer-5b4d972d-f509-5c01-8b4a-2ac3a760213f" "answer-59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" "answer-fa72f7d7-cce9-54c5-8025-23615a78b581" "answer-6c60ddf7-7739-58ba-8644-0468de1d7458"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa2daa2-a228-5079-83e2-440c95a5759c" :answer/id #uuid "2fa2daa2-a228-5079-83e2-440c95a5759c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.118" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b4d972d-f509-5c01-8b4a-2ac3a760213f" :answer/id #uuid "5b4d972d-f509-5c01-8b4a-2ac3a760213f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.123" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" :answer/id #uuid "59e161f4-bc56-55a8-80dc-2bc363955b0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.132" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa72f7d7-cce9-54c5-8025-23615a78b581" :answer/id #uuid "fa72f7d7-cce9-54c5-8025-23615a78b581" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.139" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c60ddf7-7739-58ba-8644-0468de1d7458" :answer/id #uuid "6c60ddf7-7739-58ba-8644-0468de1d7458" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.143" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ed942805-cf1d-5ddc-afc6-5eb5833ba5cd" :question/id #uuid "ed942805-cf1d-5ddc-afc6-5eb5833ba5cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-95743" :question/problem "In an airport, $58%$ of the flights arrive early. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $60$ flights are picked at random, then $40$ of them will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ | $\\,0.8997\\,$ | $\\,0.9015\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9082\\,$ | $\\,0.9099\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ | $\\,0.9162\\,$ | $\\,0.9177\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.9236\\,$ | $\\,0.9251\\,$ | $\\,0.9265\\,$ | $\\,0.9279\\,$ | $\\,0.9292\\,$ | $\\,0.9306\\,$ | $\\,0.9319\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=60$ and $p=0.58.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =60\\cdot 0.58 \\\\  & =34.8 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =60\\cdot (1-0.58) \\\\  & =25.2 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(60)(0.58)=34.8\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(60)(0.58)(1-0.58)=14.62.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(34.8,14.62).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=40).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(39.5\\le Y\\le 40.5) & =P(\\frac{39.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{40.5-34.8}{\\sqrt{14.62}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.23\\le Z\\le 1.49) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z<1.23) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.49)-P(Z\\le 1.23) \\\\  & =Φ(1.49)-Φ(1.23) \\\\  & =0.9319-0.8907 \\\\  & =0.0412\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=40)≈0.041.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0415.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-55e37936-2de1-5d34-8dd5-3c2259d1d17c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-55e37936-2de1-5d34-8dd5-3c2259d1d17c" :answer-field/id #uuid "55e37936-2de1-5d34-8dd5-3c2259d1d17c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-aa306e23-7ca1-53c8-939a-84d254417478" "answer-87044dd2-9dec-5037-baed-466b750f914c" "answer-99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" "answer-aad33263-b593-5f72-9670-762a2eec16d3" "answer-a666113c-0a9a-5270-8a84-1103dd3a6c70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa306e23-7ca1-53c8-939a-84d254417478" :answer/id #uuid "aa306e23-7ca1-53c8-939a-84d254417478" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.011" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87044dd2-9dec-5037-baed-466b750f914c" :answer/id #uuid "87044dd2-9dec-5037-baed-466b750f914c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.014" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" :answer/id #uuid "99750630-e4cc-503e-9185-a49d3e7b138b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.041" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aad33263-b593-5f72-9670-762a2eec16d3" :answer/id #uuid "aad33263-b593-5f72-9670-762a2eec16d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.141" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a666113c-0a9a-5270-8a84-1103dd3a6c70" :answer/id #uuid "a666113c-0a9a-5270-8a84-1103dd3a6c70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.401" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d886c0f0-1fbe-59f2-a61e-06cc1aef64d9" :question/id #uuid "d886c0f0-1fbe-59f2-a61e-06cc1aef64d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95775" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(120,0.6),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X=80).$ Give your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9032\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9049\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9066\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9192\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9207\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9222\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9332\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9345\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9357\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9147\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9162\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9177\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9292\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9306\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9319\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9418\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9429\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9441\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=120$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =120\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =72 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =12\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =48 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(120)(0.6)=72\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(120)(0.6)(1-0.6)=28.8.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(72,28.8).$\n\nNow, we can approximate the desired probability $P(X=80).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X=80)≈P(79.5\\le Y\\le 80.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(79.5\\le Y\\le 80.5) & =P(\\frac{79.5-72}{\\sqrt{28.8}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{80.5-72}{\\sqrt{28.8}}) \\\\  & ≈P(1.40\\le Z\\le 1.58) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.58)-P(Z<1.40) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.58)-P(Z\\le 1.40) \\\\  & =Φ(1.58)-Φ(1.40) \\\\  & =0.9429-0.9192 \\\\  & =0.0237\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X=80)≈0.024.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.0245.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6cf5633b-0c34-58f1-9af7-e4105434c5a2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cf5633b-0c34-58f1-9af7-e4105434c5a2" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cf5633b-0c34-58f1-9af7-e4105434c5a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" "answer-a4852c75-4ac7-5fb8-b281-76d948d26df8" "answer-c1275fca-a908-56a0-a111-7ff676237282" "answer-783013b8-2e23-5c9c-96aa-b7e3c5fa0e99" "answer-82c2a6d1-e3d7-55e9-b78e-a5e51b93d945"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" :answer/id #uuid "80665550-bbd8-558f-b16b-ff1eb939773c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.024" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4852c75-4ac7-5fb8-b281-76d948d26df8" :answer/id #uuid "a4852c75-4ac7-5fb8-b281-76d948d26df8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.042" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1275fca-a908-56a0-a111-7ff676237282" :answer/id #uuid "c1275fca-a908-56a0-a111-7ff676237282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.204" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783013b8-2e23-5c9c-96aa-b7e3c5fa0e99" :answer/id #uuid "783013b8-2e23-5c9c-96aa-b7e3c5fa0e99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.244" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82c2a6d1-e3d7-55e9-b78e-a5e51b93d945" :answer/id #uuid "82c2a6d1-e3d7-55e9-b78e-a5e51b93d945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.402" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-865aae44-8837-5a5c-9a79-489c7c05763a" :question/id #uuid "865aae44-8837-5a5c-9a79-489c7c05763a" :question/math-academy-id "q-132495" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(240,0.45),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X>100).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ | $\\,0.1635\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.1949\\,$ | $\\,0.1922\\,$ | $\\,0.1894\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2236\\,$ | $\\,0.2206\\,$ | $\\,0.2177\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=240$ and $p=0.45.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =240\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =108 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =240\\cdot (1-0.45) \\\\  & =132 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(240)(0.45)=108\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(240)(0.45)(1-0.45)=59.4.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(108,59.4).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>100).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>100)=P(X\\ge 101)≈P(Y\\ge 100.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 100.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{100.5-108}{\\sqrt{59.4}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge -0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.97) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.97) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.97) \\\\  & =1-0.1660 \\\\  & =0.834\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>100)≈0.834.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.8347.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbe948d3-cf3f-5d7a-a051-dde7f129c617"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbe948d3-cf3f-5d7a-a051-dde7f129c617" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbe948d3-cf3f-5d7a-a051-dde7f129c617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88f1ea9b-fe91-518b-836c-b738ddcb3eb1" "answer-dbc223ef-5e68-57a9-8abb-0d5420360510" "answer-83023c61-f5a8-5b7f-a11e-887b7484c4ba" "answer-691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" "answer-7c83485f-fc05-527b-8ac5-652edd3cc5b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88f1ea9b-fe91-518b-836c-b738ddcb3eb1" :answer/id #uuid "88f1ea9b-fe91-518b-836c-b738ddcb3eb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.805" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbc223ef-5e68-57a9-8abb-0d5420360510" :answer/id #uuid "dbc223ef-5e68-57a9-8abb-0d5420360510" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.808" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83023c61-f5a8-5b7f-a11e-887b7484c4ba" :answer/id #uuid "83023c61-f5a8-5b7f-a11e-887b7484c4ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.830" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" :answer/id #uuid "691a66a6-f8a5-5d42-a44a-c4fd56f19ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.834" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c83485f-fc05-527b-8ac5-652edd3cc5b9" :answer/id #uuid "7c83485f-fc05-527b-8ac5-652edd3cc5b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.837" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-036e3017-f74f-5cab-a417-486991609067" :question/id #uuid "036e3017-f74f-5cab-a417-486991609067" :question/math-academy-id "q-95749" :question/problem "On average, $53%$ of the emails Emily receives are spam. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that she has $70$ or more spam emails in her inbox if she has $140$ emails in her inbox. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2420\\,$ | $\\,0.2389\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=140$ and $p=0.53.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =140\\cdot 0.53 \\\\  & =74.2 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =140\\cdot (1-0.53) \\\\  & =65.8 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(140)(0.53)=74.2\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(140)(0.53)(1-0.53)=34.874.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(74.2,34.874).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 70).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈P(Y\\ge 69.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 69.5) & =1-P(Y<69.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<\\frac{69.5-74.2}{\\sqrt{34.874}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =1-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 70)≈0.788.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.7869.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ddb664b9-db89-50b9-9205-2e13e64ccf73"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ddb664b9-db89-50b9-9205-2e13e64ccf73" :answer-field/id #uuid "ddb664b9-db89-50b9-9205-2e13e64ccf73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e25c814a-f71c-51da-8fd0-0cf9e3022b1d" "answer-86dc79e8-7c69-5747-a450-f6b2d99e6665" "answer-b791b483-da31-5616-ac75-ddcf6a6d3b89" "answer-54bba7fc-c3f0-5077-b2c5-9c6593c05ac4" "answer-2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e25c814a-f71c-51da-8fd0-0cf9e3022b1d" :answer/id #uuid "e25c814a-f71c-51da-8fd0-0cf9e3022b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.718" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86dc79e8-7c69-5747-a450-f6b2d99e6665" :answer/id #uuid "86dc79e8-7c69-5747-a450-f6b2d99e6665" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.778" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b791b483-da31-5616-ac75-ddcf6a6d3b89" :answer/id #uuid "b791b483-da31-5616-ac75-ddcf6a6d3b89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.781" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54bba7fc-c3f0-5077-b2c5-9c6593c05ac4" :answer/id #uuid "54bba7fc-c3f0-5077-b2c5-9c6593c05ac4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.787" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c" :answer/id #uuid "2ec81fad-4d2f-5c95-95d4-c12a0665f84c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.788" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fff943eb-16aa-5bd0-bfca-c95efa66ad92" :question/id #uuid "fff943eb-16aa-5bd0-bfca-c95efa66ad92" :question/math-academy-id "q-132501" :question/problem "In a biased coin, $60%$ of the flips land on heads. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that the coin lands on heads at least $21$ times out of $50$ flips. Give your final answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0017\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ | $\\,0.0016\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0023\\,$ | $\\,0.0022\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0032\\,$ | $\\,0.0031\\,$ | $\\,0.0030\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p=0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(50)(0.6)=30\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(50)(0.6)(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(30,12).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 21).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈P(Y>20.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y>20.5) & =1-P(Y\\le 20.5) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le \\frac{20.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(Z\\le -2.74) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.74) \\\\  & =1-0.0031 \\\\  & =0.9969\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 21)≈0.997.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.9966.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e23856fd-2bc7-50d7-8813-dede2be61e2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e23856fd-2bc7-50d7-8813-dede2be61e2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e23856fd-2bc7-50d7-8813-dede2be61e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e2bc3d2-c9db-5dcc-a332-edf3d005e87a" "answer-cf962d0a-ba4f-5d4a-9cdf-86447e6df013" "answer-00211791-98ae-5ba1-b2e7-8f6918fccada" "answer-594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" "answer-ebc689f5-84ba-5d2f-8983-18b666da6119"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2bc3d2-c9db-5dcc-a332-edf3d005e87a" :answer/id #uuid "6e2bc3d2-c9db-5dcc-a332-edf3d005e87a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.991" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf962d0a-ba4f-5d4a-9cdf-86447e6df013" :answer/id #uuid "cf962d0a-ba4f-5d4a-9cdf-86447e6df013" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.993" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00211791-98ae-5ba1-b2e7-8f6918fccada" :answer/id #uuid "00211791-98ae-5ba1-b2e7-8f6918fccada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.995" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" :answer/id #uuid "594d08e2-0822-590c-8d72-5d55e33b28f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.997" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebc689f5-84ba-5d2f-8983-18b666da6119" :answer/id #uuid "ebc689f5-84ba-5d2f-8983-18b666da6119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.999" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-01c1e9f3-92c7-5436-aa3f-7f0248f68a95" :question/id #uuid "01c1e9f3-92c7-5436-aa3f-7f0248f68a95" :question/math-academy-id "q-95816" :question/problem "Given that the random variable $X∼B(70,0.52),$ use the normal approximation to the binomial distribution to approximate $P(X\\ge 37).$ Round your final answer to three decimal places\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.4602\\,$ | $\\,0.4562\\,$ | $\\,0.4522\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.0\\,$ | $\\,0.5000\\,$ | $\\,0.5040\\,$ | $\\,0.5080\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=70$ and $p=0.52.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =70\\cdot 0.52 \\\\  & =36.4 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =70\\cdot (1-0.52) \\\\  & =33.6 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(70)(0.52)=36.4\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(70)(0.52)(1-0.52)=17.472.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(36.4,17.472).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X\\ge 37).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈P(Y\\ge 36.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 36.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{36.5-36.4}{\\sqrt{17.472}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.02) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.02) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.02) \\\\  & =1-0.5080 \\\\  & =0.4920\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 37)≈0.492.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.4905.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0750819-f5ed-5c61-8873-54bb6b64a700"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0750819-f5ed-5c61-8873-54bb6b64a700" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0750819-f5ed-5c61-8873-54bb6b64a700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-386ffdee-0a9c-5c48-9620-f36a06ff13fd" "answer-4c710fdf-cb10-5d92-8c25-86c325e7bc75" "answer-df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" "answer-8d7ddccf-b8ca-503f-bee5-847a976cfb15" "answer-bb429d2f-5684-55ac-95e1-cd71db6ea25d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-386ffdee-0a9c-5c48-9620-f36a06ff13fd" :answer/id #uuid "386ffdee-0a9c-5c48-9620-f36a06ff13fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.473" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c710fdf-cb10-5d92-8c25-86c325e7bc75" :answer/id #uuid "4c710fdf-cb10-5d92-8c25-86c325e7bc75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.487" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" :answer/id #uuid "df7f9485-4dc8-5660-afc2-33f733c110d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.492" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d7ddccf-b8ca-503f-bee5-847a976cfb15" :answer/id #uuid "8d7ddccf-b8ca-503f-bee5-847a976cfb15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.503" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb429d2f-5684-55ac-95e1-cd71db6ea25d" :answer/id #uuid "bb429d2f-5684-55ac-95e1-cd71db6ea25d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.511" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-212cd403-0726-5b1b-ae69-c8952ab761bd" :question/id #uuid "212cd403-0726-5b1b-ae69-c8952ab761bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-132503" :question/problem "In a math test, a student has a $55%$ probability of correctly answering each question. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, estimate the probability that a student correctly solves more than $10$ questions of a $20$-question test. Give your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3446\\,$ | $\\,0.3409\\,$ | $\\,0.3372\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3821\\,$ | $\\,0.3783\\,$ | $\\,0.3745\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.4207\\,$ | $\\,0.4168\\,$ | $\\,0.4129\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a binomial random variable $X∼B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p)>5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu =np$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY∼N(np,np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=20$ and $p=0.55.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =20\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =11 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =20\\cdot (1-0.55) \\\\  & =9 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu =np=(20)(0.55)=11\n$$\n\nand variance\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=np(1-p)=(20)(0.55)(1-0.55)=4.95.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by $Y∼N(11,4.95).$\n\nNow, we can estimate the desired probability $P(X>10).$ Using a continuity correction, we have\n\n$$\nP(X>10)=P(X\\ge 11)≈P(Y\\ge 10.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 10.5) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{10.5-11}{\\sqrt{4.95}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge -0.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<-0.22) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -0.22) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.22) \\\\  & =1-0.4129 \\\\  & =0.5871\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X>10)≈0.587.\n$$\n\nNote: Using a software package for the approximation gives a probability of $0.5914.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-00cb2415-3f5b-552e-bb6f-9f456d752a78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00cb2415-3f5b-552e-bb6f-9f456d752a78" :answer-field/id #uuid "00cb2415-3f5b-552e-bb6f-9f456d752a78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd5d40eb-d6c1-59c7-b0ed-ebf1039c7633" "answer-35350a1f-7895-51fc-8e71-c814db467aef" "answer-54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" "answer-65d9e86c-9f69-5f93-b937-a18b030341ef" "answer-d9bdf78a-e3f2-5b0b-8bcf-024af2813423"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd5d40eb-d6c1-59c7-b0ed-ebf1039c7633" :answer/id #uuid "dd5d40eb-d6c1-59c7-b0ed-ebf1039c7633" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.579" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35350a1f-7895-51fc-8e71-c814db467aef" :answer/id #uuid "35350a1f-7895-51fc-8e71-c814db467aef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.583" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" :answer/id #uuid "54feab7b-9521-5920-abd6-97ca8ad85f0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.587" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65d9e86c-9f69-5f93-b937-a18b030341ef" :answer/id #uuid "65d9e86c-9f69-5f93-b937-a18b030341ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.626" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9bdf78a-e3f2-5b0b-8bcf-024af2813423" :answer/id #uuid "d9bdf78a-e3f2-5b0b-8bcf-024af2813423" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.663" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116536 :tutorial/content "Suppose the random variable $X\\sim B(n,p)$ follows a binomial distribution.\n\nThere are certain situations where working with binomial random variables becomes difficult. For example, when the parameter $n$ is large, computing exact probabilities can become problematic due to the large numbers involved.\n\nUnder certain conditions, we can approximate the *discrete* random variable $X$ using a *continuous normal* random variable $Y,$ given by\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(\\mu, \\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ are calculated using the formulas for the mean and variance of the binomial random variable $X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mu = np, \\qquad \\sigma^2 = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTo determine whether a given binomial distribution can be approximated using a normal distribution, we use the following rule of thumb:\n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5.$\n\nTo get a feel for how this works, let's consider a few examples of binomial distributions and their corresponding normal approximations:\n\n- First, consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.2.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{1}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{0.8}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = 1 < 5,\\qquad n(1-p) = 4 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{0.8}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.2).$ \n\nThe diagram below compares the two distributions. The yellow rectangles correspond to the values of the PMF of $B(5, 0.2),$ and the smooth blue curve is the PDF of the corresponding normal distribution.\n\n![](images/6a/6a67b3ad65bfedcd25b787ff15e72c575da1215ae47502ff11f97d40de407560.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the case where $n=5$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{2.5}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{1.25}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 2.5 < 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np = n(1-p) < 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{2.5}},{\\color{red}{1.25}})$ is *not* a good approximation to $B(5, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/0a/0a8f2d9ccd78ee885ea3bb8f2f00705f69c520f8ec0588411865399b6ecef046.png)\n\n- Finally, we consider the case where $n=20$ and $p=0.5.$ In this case, it's straightforward to show that \n\n$$\n\\mu = {\\color{blue}{10}},\\qquad \\sigma^2 = {\\color{red}{5}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nnp = n(1-p) = 10 > 5.\n$$\n\nSince $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5,$ the normal distribution $N({\\color{blue}{10}},{\\color{red}{5}})$ *is* a good approximation to $B(20, 0.5).$ \n\n![](images/9e/9efc7df7c423ed2d8d6cc70ef820de3cb235f644567a3fec0d0a0e501042e305.png)\n\nThe fact that we can approximate binomial random variables as normal when $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5$ is a consequence of the so-called **central limit theorem** (or CLT). We'll discuss this in more detail at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116542 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a random variable $X \\sim B(50,0.6).$ Let's estimate the value of $P(X=33)$ using the normal approximation.\n\nGiven a binomial random variable $X \\sim B(n,p),$ if \n\n- $np > 5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-p) > 5,$\n\nwe can approximate $X$ as a normal random variable $Y$ with mean $\\mu = np$ and variance $\\sigma^2 = np(1-p),$ that is, \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(np, np(1-p)).\n$$\n\nHere, we have $n=50$ and $p = 0.6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}np & =50\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =30 \\\\  & >5\\,✓ \\\\ n(1-p) & =50\\cdot (1-0.6) \\\\  & =20 \\\\  & >5.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can approximate our binomial random variable $X$ with a normal random variable $Y$ with mean\n\n$$\n\\mu = 50\\cdot0.6 = 30\n$$\n\nand variance \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = 50\\cdot0.6\\cdot(1-0.6)=12.\n$$\n\nThat is, we can approximate $X$ by \n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12).\n$$\n\nNow, since $X$ is discrete and $Y$ is continuous, we need to apply a continuity correction. The required continuity correction in this case is\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5).\n$$\n\nTo compute this probability, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =P(\\frac{32.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{33.5-30}{\\sqrt{12}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0.72\\le Z\\le 1.01) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z<0.72) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.01)-P(Z\\le 0.72) \\\\  & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a table of values for the CDF of the standard normal distribution, we find that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(1.01) = 0.8438, \\qquad \\Phi(0.72) = 0.7642.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(32.5\\le Y\\le 33.5) & =Φ(1.01)-Φ(0.72) \\\\  & =0.8438-0.7642 \\\\  & =0.0796.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx 0.0796.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116543 :tutorial/content "In the last example, we approximated the binomial random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim B(50,0.6)\n$$\n\nusing a normal random variable\n\n$$\nY \\sim N(30,12),\n$$\n\nand we arrived at the approximation\n\n$$\nP(X=33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796.\n$$\n\nIf you were to use a software package to evaluate this approximation, you might get an answer like\n\n$$\nP(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0791.\n$$\n\nNotice that there is a difference of around $0.0005$ between the two answers.\n\nThere is a reason for this discrepancy. When we calculated that $P(X = 33) \\approx P(32.5 \\leq Y \\leq 33.5)  \\approx 0.0796,$ we rounded our $z$-values two decimal places and then used a lookup table to find $\\Phi(z)$ for those rounded values. As a result, we introduced some small rounding errors. \n\nTypically, the rounding errors are small, so we will continue to work with a table of values. However, it's worth bearing this in mind if you're using a software package or calculator to compute $\\Phi(z).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186116546 :tutorial/content "According to the so-called **central limit theorem**, if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots X_n$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) random variables, then\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx N(n\\mu, n\\sigma^2),\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is \"sufficiently large,\" and\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2.\n$$\n\nNow, if $X\\sim B(n,p),$ then $X$ can be thought of as a sum of $n$ I.I.D Bernoulli random variables \n\n$$\nX_i\\sim\\textrm{Bernoulli}(p),\n$$\n\nwhere the PMF of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n![](images/13/134fcb82e69ef8d5fc1e63353f3b28c036bda08176918bcd166ebc52826854c6.svg)\n\nIt can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\mu = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\sigma^2 = p(1-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting our values of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$ into our normal approximation for $X,$ we get\n\n$$\nX \\approx N\\left(np, np(1-p)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe \"sufficiently large\" condition on $n$ is taken care of by stipulating that $np > 5$ and $n(1-p) > 5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- When $p\\approx 0.5,$ then $n\\approx 10$ is sufficiently large for the normal approximation to be applied.\n\n- However, if $p$ is closer to zero or one, then the distribution of $X$ is not symmetric, and consequently, we need $n$ to be much larger for the central limit theorem to be valid." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186115440 :activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186083837 :question/answer-fields 17592186081953] {:db/id 17592186083200 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-01e135d3-05cc-5121-8735-150ba42cbf5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-01e135d3-05cc-5121-8735-150ba42cbf5d" :question/id #uuid "01e135d3-05cc-5121-8735-150ba42cbf5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-118683" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=2x-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=2x-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{(2x-3)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{{u}^{-4}}{-4}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[1-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-46da6bbf-df72-58cf-ab47-22a431cba277"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-46da6bbf-df72-58cf-ab47-22a431cba277" :answer-field/id #uuid "46da6bbf-df72-58cf-ab47-22a431cba277" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" :answer-field/choices ["answer-34be0800-1b32-5b90-8444-e88edf6c9a34" "answer-1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" "answer-72662bf2-ec94-5cdc-8665-4b665b80f3e5" "answer-47fea95f-c75b-556a-9356-f42d7572185d" "answer-16ccf152-39ad-58ab-b622-b32a7cb53d17"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34be0800-1b32-5b90-8444-e88edf6c9a34" :answer/id #uuid "34be0800-1b32-5b90-8444-e88edf6c9a34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" :answer/id #uuid "1449b75d-0e64-501c-8d2a-cce137f4b608" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72662bf2-ec94-5cdc-8665-4b665b80f3e5" :answer/id #uuid "72662bf2-ec94-5cdc-8665-4b665b80f3e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47fea95f-c75b-556a-9356-f42d7572185d" :answer/id #uuid "47fea95f-c75b-556a-9356-f42d7572185d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16ccf152-39ad-58ab-b622-b32a7cb53d17" :answer/id #uuid "16ccf152-39ad-58ab-b622-b32a7cb53d17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e3a99356-f2d9-5da7-917f-fe8d47cb0308"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e3a99356-f2d9-5da7-917f-fe8d47cb0308" :question/id #uuid "e3a99356-f2d9-5da7-917f-fe8d47cb0308" :question/math-academy-id "q-152780" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=3t-2.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $t\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=3t-2$ is as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $4$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(3t-2)^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{5}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{4}^{a}{u}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{u}^{-3/2}]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{(a)^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{(4)^{3/2}}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc8e448d-ca79-54b8-bab8-a1334af95399"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc8e448d-ca79-54b8-bab8-a1334af95399" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc8e448d-ca79-54b8-bab8-a1334af95399" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" "answer-e02b37dc-ebae-57b4-af31-87e189cd4c3f" "answer-695c0ea1-77ec-56ce-a589-ce5482b85acc" "answer-ed16ccaa-3b79-5f36-a20f-c1259e6c3bd9" "answer-13aeb1d6-3a6d-5d19-bcaa-cfdc021ab9c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" :answer/id #uuid "b03dad7b-0e07-5cb2-b401-99a18df64f52" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e02b37dc-ebae-57b4-af31-87e189cd4c3f" :answer/id #uuid "e02b37dc-ebae-57b4-af31-87e189cd4c3f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-695c0ea1-77ec-56ce-a589-ce5482b85acc" :answer/id #uuid "695c0ea1-77ec-56ce-a589-ce5482b85acc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed16ccaa-3b79-5f36-a20f-c1259e6c3bd9" :answer/id #uuid "ed16ccaa-3b79-5f36-a20f-c1259e6c3bd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13aeb1d6-3a6d-5d19-bcaa-cfdc021ab9c3" :answer/id #uuid "13aeb1d6-3a6d-5d19-bcaa-cfdc021ab9c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9628825a-c202-5d36-bd75-c73eb4a112e0" :activity/id #uuid "9628825a-c202-5d36-bd75-c73eb4a112e0" :activity/title "Improper Integrals" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity